@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+## Deploying Fizzy with Kamal
+
+Fizzy includes a single-server [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/) configuration in
+[`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml). It runs the web app, Solid Queue
+background jobs, SQLite databases, and local file storage in one container. The
+only other container on the server is `kamal-proxy`, which provides HTTPS and
+routes traffic to Fizzy.
+
+### Requirements
+
+- A Linux server with Docker and SSH access. Kamal can install Docker for you
+  if the server is reachable as `root`.
+- Docker on the machine you deploy from.
+- DNS for your Fizzy hostname pointing at the server.
+- Ports 80 and 443 open on the server so Let's Encrypt can issue a certificate.
+- An SMTP server that will relay mail for your Fizzy hostname.
+
+### Configure the server and hostname
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml` and replace the placeholders:
+
+1. `servers.web` — the IP address or hostname of your server.
+2. `proxy.host` — the public hostname, for example `fizzy.example.com`.
+3. `APP_HOST` under `env.clear` — the same public hostname.
+4. `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, and
+   `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` — your mail server settings. `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO`
+   defaults to `"true"`, which is correct for most mail servers on port 587.
+5. `builder.arch` — `amd64` for most servers. Change it to `arm64` for an ARM
+   server.
+
+The configuration uses Kamal's local registry. Kamal starts a registry on the
+machine you deploy from and tunnels it to the server, so you don't need a
+separate hosted container registry or registry credentials.
+
+### Configure secrets
+
+Secrets are read from `.kamal/secrets`, which references environment variables
+so it remains safe to commit. Set the following in your shell or password
+manager before deploying:
+
+```sh
+# Generate this once and keep it stable across deployments. Changing it will
+# invalidate existing sessions and other signed data.
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE="$(bin/rails secret)"
+
+# Optional: only needed if you use Rails encrypted credentials.
+# export RAILS_MASTER_KEY="$(cat config/master.key)"
+
+export SMTP_PASSWORD="your-smtp-password"
+
+# Optional: web push notification keys. Generate a pair with:
+#   bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}"'
+export VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY="..."
+export VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY="..."
+```
+
+If you prefer, update `.kamal/secrets` to fetch these from your password manager
+instead. Never put raw credentials in the repository.
+
+### Deploy
+
+The first deployment installs Docker if needed, starts `kamal-proxy`, builds the
+image, and boots Fizzy:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Once it finishes, visit `https://your-host`. Sign up with your email address;
+the magic-link email is delivered through the SMTP server you configured.
+
+Subsequent deployments use the same named volume and preserve all application
+data:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+### Data and background jobs
+
+The named Docker volume `fizzy` is mounted at `/rails/storage`. It contains all
+SQLite databases (primary, cache, queue, and cable), uploads, and other local
+storage. It is intentionally not replaced during deployment. Back this volume
+up regularly.
+
+Solid Queue runs inside the Puma process (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=true`), so
+background jobs run on the same box and in the same container as the web app.
+No separate worker container is required.
